    "result_text": "Both functions are implemented in `app/network.py`. `pytest -q` passes (3 tests), but those tests don't exercise the new code, so I also checked it with a script that ran against a stub transport:\n\n- Requests carried `X-Acme-Job: nightly-sync` alongside huggingface_hub's own user agent, with timeouts of connect 5s and read/write/pool 60s.\n- A connection failure and a read timeout each logged a warning on `app.network` and returned `None`.\n- A 404 for a missing repo still raised `RepositoryNotFoundError`.\n\n**How it works**\n\n- **`configure_hub_http(transport=None)`** registers a client factory with huggingface_hub's `set_client_factory`, so every `HfApi` instance shares one client that has our timeouts, header and transport. It also re-attaches huggingface_hub's own request hook, which enforces offline mode (`HF_HUB_OFFLINE`) and adds request IDs. That hook isn't exported publicly, so I import it from `huggingface_hub.utils._http`, which could break in a future release.\n- **`current_revision(repo_id)`** returns `HfApi().model_info(repo_id, revision=\"main\").sha`. It catches only connection failures and timeouts; every other error, including HTTP errors, still raises.\n\n**A problem I had to work around:** `model_info` and several other `HfApi` methods pass `timeout=None` on each request by default. In the HTTP library huggingface_hub 2.0.0 uses (`httpx2`, not `httpx`), that turns off all timeouts rather than falling back to the client's. A normal client would therefore have ignored our 5s/60s settings. The shared client is a small subclass that treats a `None` timeout as \"use the client default\", so the timeouts always apply. A caller who passes a real timeout still gets that value.\n\n**Decision for you:** I only treat connection failures and timeouts as \"unreachable\". A connection dropped after it was established (for example a reset during the read) will still raise. If you want that returned as `None` too, I can widen the catch to `httpx2.NetworkError`.\n\nI wasn't allow"
  },
